    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=4 y$ and $Q(x,y)=- 4 x$\n- $P(x,y)=\\frac{x}{2} + \\cos{\\left(y^{4} \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{y}{2}$\n- $P(x,y)=0$ and $Q(x,y)=x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I does *not* give the required area. In this case, \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=4$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-4,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(4\\right)=\\left(-4\\right)-\\left(4\\right)=-8\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=- 4 y^{3} \\sin{\\left(y^{4} \\right)}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=0,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y^{3} \\sin{\\left(y^{4} \\right)}\\right)=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- 4 y^{3} \\sin{\\left(y^{4} \\right)}\\right)=4 y^{3} \\sin{\\left(y^{4} \\right)}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/id": "4d9ada50-4b44-53dd-9064-8cb5bdf9d843",
