  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186133895,
      "id": "5f4f726e-d985-5915-a4d0-30cfa6f4b89b",
      "title": "Identifying Vector Fields That Satisfy the Unit Curl Property"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Let $C$ be a simple, piecewise-smooth, closed, and positively oriented curve. Given that the line integral\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\n$$\n\n \nrepresents the area of the region $D$ enclosed by any such curve in ${R}^{2},$ which of the following could be the functions $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y)?$\n\n- $P(x,y)=- \\frac{y}{3} + e^{x + y}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\frac{2 x}{3} + e^{x + y}$\n- $P(x,y)=5 \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}$ and $Q(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$\n- $P(x,y)=x$ and $Q(x,y)=x$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "According to Green's theorem, if \n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=1,\n$$\n\nwe can write the area of the region $D$ enclosed by the curve $C$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Area}(D) & ={∬}_{D}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∮}_{C}P\\,\\text{d}x+Q\\,\\text{d}y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each pair in turn.\n\n- Pair I gives the required area.  In this case,\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=e^{x + y} - \\frac{1}{3}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=e^{x + y} + \\frac{2}{3},$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(e^{x + y} + \\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(e^{x + y} - \\frac{1}{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)+\\left(e^{x + y}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(e^{x + y}\\right)=1.\n$$\n\n- Pair II does *not* give the required area.  Notice that \n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=5 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\right)-\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)=\\left(- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)}\\right)-\\left(5 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\right)=- \\sin{\\left(x \\right)} - 5 \\cos{\\left(y \\right)}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\n- Pair III gives the required area. Notice that\n$\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=0$\nand\n$\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=1,$\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=\\left(1\\right)-\\left(0\\right)=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
